<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.8.3: Writing Equations and Isolating Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00190</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.3: Writing Equations and Isolating Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>793b8667-e78a-4b1f-bf29-296d46943648</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Use the following scenario for questions 1 – 7:</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Tank A initially contained 124 liters of water. It is then filled with more water, at a constant rate of 9 liters per minute.</para>
      <exercise id="_02cd7ec6-2c93-4a63-8c84-579687fa77e9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">How many liters of water are in Tank A after 4 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Correct! 160</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Nice try! The correct answer is 160</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_7cca0ed8-6958-4125-a692-7aefdee8e06b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">How many liters of water are in Tank A after 80 seconds?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Correct! 136</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Nice try! The correct answer is 136</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">How many liters of water are in Tank A after 4 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>124</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="_64f729a2-90bb-4753-9f2c-0cbf4faf3a14" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">How many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, have passed when Tank A contains 151 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Correct! 3</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Nice try! The correct answer is 3</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_58431f0d-4e03-4eab-83d6-6a8fba7eebfb" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">How many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, have passed when Tank A contains 191.5 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Correct! 7.5</para>
          <para id="para-00017">Nice try! The correct answer is 7.5</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_18a4e338-8371-4387-8b0b-404af3e07d5d" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">How many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, have passed when Tank A contains 270.25 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00019">Correct! 16.25</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Nice try! The correct answer is 16.25</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00021">How many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, have passed when Tank A contains <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>124</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00024">For questions 8 – 14, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para id="para-00025">Tank B, which initially contained 80 liters of water, is being drained at a rate of 2.5 liters per minute.</para>
      <exercise id="b69ebe38-b909-4b91-a2be-57d87cd67ce9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00026">How many liters of water are in Tank B after 30 seconds?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Correct! 78.75</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Nice try! The correct answer is 78.75</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="c8613b4f-ce83-4e05-afbe-280478f7b8ac" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00029">How many liters of water are in Tank B after 7 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00030">Correct! 62.5</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Nice try! The correct answer is 62.5</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00032">How many liters of water are in Tank B after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>80</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="faf4b7e6-5040-40d6-acb4-8942ac268566" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00035">For how many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, has the water been draining when Tank B contains 75 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00036">Correct! 2</para>
          <para id="para-00037">Nice try! The correct answer is 2</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5a22dfc4-28bd-4658-86b9-60e3bb15bc01" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00038">For how many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, has the water been draining when Tank B contains 32.5 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00039">Correct! 19</para>
          <para id="para-00040">Nice try! The correct answer is 19</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_4b4843d7-b07d-4bb1-ab87-dd68b284af61" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00041">For how many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, has the water been draining when Tank B contains 18 liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00042">Correct! 24.8</para>
          <para id="para-00043">Nice try! The correct answer is 24.8</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q14-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <para id="para-00044">For how many minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, has the water been draining when Tank B contains v liters of water?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00045">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>80</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>v</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <!--Interaction
      End -->
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Isolating the Variable</title>
      <para id="para-00047">Watch the following video to further explore the above situation.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b3e1617f9209d3d2ce606e25ab6db467025d0a42"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00048">A pool is filled with 15,000 gallons of water. It loses 200 gallons per day. How many days, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, have passed when the pool has <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gallons of water?</para>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Choosing Which Equation to Use</title>
        <para id="para-00049">A relationship between quantities can be described in more than one way. Some ways are more helpful than others, depending on what we want to find out. Let’s look at the angles of an isosceles triangle, for example.</para>
        
          <media alt="Triangle with angles labeled a, b, and a.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_10_08_01.png"/>
          </media>
        
        <para id="para-00050">The two angles near the horizontal side have equal measurement in degrees, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. The sum of angles in a triangle is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>∘</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> , so the relationship between the angles can be expressed as: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <section id="sect-00006">
          <title>Example 1</title>
          <para id="para-00051">Suppose we want to find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo>∘</m:mo></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>160</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></section>
        <section id="sect-00007">
          <title>Example 2</title>
          <para id="para-00054">What is the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> if <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mn>72.5</m:mn><m:mo>∘</m:mo></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>72.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>72.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>145</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>35</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00056">Notice that when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is given, we did the same calculation repeatedly to find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: We substituted <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> into the first equation, subtracted <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> from 180, and then divided the result by 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00057">Instead of taking these steps over and over whenever we know <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and want to find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, we can rearrange the equation to isolate <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>90</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00059">This equation is equivalent to the first one. To find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, we can now simply substitute any value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> into this equation and evaluate the expression on the right side.</para>
          <para id="para-00060">Likewise, we can write an equivalent equation to make it easier to find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when we know <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00062">Rearranging an equation to isolate one variable is called solving for a variable. In this example, we have solved for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. All three equations are equivalent. Depending on what information we have and what we are interested in, we can choose a particular equation to use.</para>
        </section>
        <!--Q#-->
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00063">Rewrite the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> as an equivalent equation that could be used to easily find <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00064">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>180</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Choosing Which Equation to Use</title>
          <!--Q#-->
          <exercise id="exer-00006">
            <problem id="prob-00016">
              <para id="para-00066">Jake and Bryce are saving their allowance. Jake saved for one week, and Bryce saved for 2 weeks. Together they saved $15. Write an equation to find Bryce’s allowance.</para>
              <para id="para-00067">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00016">
              <para id="para-00068">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00069">Here is how to write an equation to find Bryce’s allowance.</para>
              <para id="para-00070">Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: Jake’s allowance and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: Bryce’s allowance.</para>
              <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00072">Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to find Bryce’s allowance.</para>
              <para id="para-00073"><emphasis>STEP 1:</emphasis> Subtract <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> from each side. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>j</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00074"><emphasis>Step 2:</emphasis> Simplify. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 3:</emphasis> Divide both sides by 2. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00076">So to find Bryce’s allowance, use the equation: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>